**Testimonial: The Introduction**  
*David R., 40, Foreign Service Officer, Washington D.C.*  
*The Peninsula, Hong Kong, September 2023*

I met her at a hotel bar in Mid-Levels—this tiny Filipino woman barely five feet tall, wearing a fitted yellow dress that showed off her golden legs. But it was her eyes that stopped me. Dark brown, almost black, captivating in their own reserved way. She looked at me with that guarded composure, giving nothing away, yet I knew immediately I wanted her.

I bought her white roses and we were upstairs within the hour. I put my hand on her midriff and felt her tremble beneath my fingers—this reserved domestic helper who had never stepped out of line. I guided her to the bed and she gave herself to me completely, her dark eyes wide and vulnerable as I made love to her. Afterward, she cried in my arms—not from regret, but from release. That was when she told me about the fiancé in Phoenix. The contractor who expected her to fly to America in three weeks, marry him, live a small life.

I convinced her that night to break it off. She called him from my bathroom, her voice shaking, and when she hung up she collapsed against my shoulder sobbing. I held her, stroked her back, told her she deserved more than gratitude and a green card. I told her she had a gift—this body, these captivating eyes, this tolerance for men's gross habits. I knew people. I could get her a job.

The next morning I made some calls. By afternoon she had an interview with an escort company that catered to diplomats and businessmen. They loved her—the 5-foot frame, the olive skin, the way she could cook Filipino food and pack a suitcase and watch basketball while caressing a man's ego. She quit her domestic contract that day.

What followed was two weeks of the purest girlfriend experience I've ever had. She wore her casual dresses to sleep in my bed, let me caress her Filipino legs while we watched TV, did my laundry without question, cooked me adobo that tasted like home. She tolerated my wet towels, my snoring, my temper. She shopped efficiently, got pedicures that I paid for, and every morning there were white roses on the nightstand. She was reserved with others but open with me—those dark brown eyes looking up at me with gratitude and something like love, though we both knew the math.

I taught her how to be a professional. How to kiss with just enough hesitation to feel real. How to tremble on command when a hand touched her midriff. How to cry softly if a client needed to feel like he was rescuing her. She was a natural.

I left for Tokyo on day fourteen. She stayed at The Peninsula, now working for the escort company, no longer a helper but a companion. Last I heard, she never did get on that plane to Phoenix. She sends me photos sometimes—her in new dresses, her pedicured feet, that captivating look in her dark eyes. I keep a white rose in my diplomatic passport. I tell myself I saved her from a life of drudgery. Sometimes I believe it.
